---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { APP_NAME } from "@/lib/appConfig";

export const prerender = true;
// No `.reveal` here: that class starts at opacity 0 and only index.astro runs
// the observer that reveals it. `manifesto-body` is likewise index-scoped
// (it subtracts the plate's wave bands); its 5rem / 7rem rhythm is used directly.
---

<Layout title={`Backstory — ${APP_NAME}`} description={`Where ${APP_NAME} came from.`} noindex>
  <main class="bg-surface font-body text-base relative flex flex-1 flex-col">
    <section class="bg-blue flex-1">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
        <h1
          class="font-hero text-[clamp(2.5rem,5vw,3.4rem)] leading-[1.05] tracking-tight text-light"
        >
          Backstory
        </h1>
        {
          /* <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/85">
          It's hard to know where to find public drinking fountains, and even harder to know if
          they're working.
        </p> */
        }
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/85">
          It all begins on one muggy summer day in Washington D.C. I had just moved to DC and was
          (naively) running without a waterbottle. As the sun is getting the better of me, I see a
          fountain in the distance. Nothing happens when I press the button, but this is fine
          because I can actually see <em>another</em> fountain like 400 meters away! When this fountain{
            " "
          }
          <i>and the next one</i> don't work though, I realize I'll need to make preparations before my
          next run. Little did I know that <i>this</i> is what preparations would look like.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/85">
          DC's an especially interesting case-study because it occupies a middleground where there
          are enough fountains that there's likely one near you but not enough that there's
          typically redundancy protecting you from a broken fountain. If you're anywhere other than
          the National Mall, finding a working fountain is not a likelihood you'd want to put money
          on.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/85">
          Now some other good folks have taken a swing at this problem. In particular, there are a
          handful of pretty decent maps on Reddit. They're a good, but they're hampered not by the
          people but by the tooling. These citizen mappers are given little choice other than the
          very generalized tools of Big Map—good for every use case but great for none. This leads
          to downstream consequences: the map is hard to read because it was built with a
          generalized tool, the pace and reliability of updates are usually bottlenecked by the
          person who created the map, there is no node-level history of map updates, when limited
          amount of detail that can be added to a node, bottlenecked crowd sourcing information
          about fountainsOr maybe you've found a map some folks made together, but when you arrive
          at the fountain, it's either out of order or <em>even removed</em>! This unreliability
          delivers an experience similar to not even having public fountains in the first place, and
          that's no good for anyone. It's hard to know where to find public drinking fountains. It's
          even harder to know if they're working or—in the winter—if they're running.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
